Laughter inspired by brutality is made possible 设n eons-old convention.
Since the dawn of storytelling, artists have maintained a bright line between
drama and 听 by controlling ル’s perception of pain: In true
drama, everybody gets hurt; in true 听, nobody gets hurt. Not really.
Comic characters may writhe, scream, bounce off walls, and tear their
hair out, 目y do it か wild spirit that allows 看
to sit back, laugh, and safely feel that it doesn’t really hurt. For without a
clear comic style, 看 would naturally feel sorry for
suffering characters. To the comic writer, empathy spells death. Compassion
kills laughs. Therefore, comic 及 must keep り/„
cool, critical, unempathetic—Đ safe side of pain.
Here’列 short list of four 及s designed to keep 例
distance and trigger laughs.
